{"id": "q0225", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3936810795, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "923cbbdfe3213927dfa36b1f8e0eb48a30d641d9b9bf7cf25d16765340d88836", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 7, "query": "Assume that all gases are perfect and that data refer to 298.15 K unless otherwise stated. Calculate the change in chemical potential of a perfect gas when its pressure is increased isothermally from $1.8 \\mathrm{~atm}$ to $29.5 \\mathrm{~atm}$ at $40^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.\n\nA. +10.2$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nB. +7.3$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nC. +8.4$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nD. +1.9$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nE. +5.9$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nF. +2.6$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nG. +6.2$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nH. +4.8$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nI. +3.5$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nJ. +9.1$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0225.wav", "input_sha256": "58f3ac33eb0a0b30608a603f8b6bfbb0dbedd74d1fb76b26d000d1f3bfe00024", "input_samples": 779200, "input_s": 48.7, "n_real_chunks": 49, "score": 0.8175, "act": 0.9996, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "All right, so we're asked to calculate the change in chemical potential of a perfect gas when its pressure is increased isotherm ally from 1.8 atm to 29.5 at m at 4 0°C. So first, let' s recall that the chemical potential of a perfect gas is given by the equation mu equal s RT natural log of P over R T plus constant. Since the temperature is constant, the change in chemical potential will only depend on the change in pressure. So we can write delta mu equal s R T natural log of P final over P initial. Now, we' re given the initial and final pressures, as well as the temperature. Let's convert the temperature to Kelvin. 40° C plus 27 3.15 equal s 3 13. 15 K. The gas constant R is approximately 8. 314 J per mole Kelvin. Plugging these values into the equation, we get delta mu equal s 8.31", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 17, "eot_seen": false, "n_speak_chunks": 60, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0225_local_local.wav", "sha256": "db1a73e08c5711d1a413f04134e8401f6dcce99e58993f6d0dd62751cb076860", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 60.0}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.058, 1.104, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.057, 2.102, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 3.0, 3.059, 3.105, 1, 0, 0], [3, 1, 4.0, 4.06, 4.105, 1, 0, 0], [4, 1, 5.0, 5.06, 5.108, 1, 0, 0], [5, 1, 6.0, 6.059, 6.105, 1, 0, 0], [6, 1, 7.0, 7.067, 7.12, 1, 0, 0], [7, 1, 8.0, 8.068, 8.122, 1, 0, 0], [8, 1, 9.0, 9.068, 9.121, 1, 0, 0], [9, 1, 10.0, 10.059, 10.106, 1, 0, 0], [10, 1, 11.0, 11.06, 11.107, 1, 0, 0], [11, 1, 12.0, 12.061, 12.107, 1, 0, 0], [12, 1, 13.0, 13.061, 13.107, 1, 0, 0], [13, 1, 14.0, 14.07, 14.123, 1, 0, 0], [14, 1, 15.0, 15.065, 15.114, 1, 0, 0], [15, 1, 16.0, 16.065, 16.113, 1, 0, 0], [16, 1, 17.0, 17.07, 17.123, 1, 0, 0], [17, 1, 18.0, 18.065, 18.61, 0, 24000, 0], [18, 1, 19.0, 19.065, 19.651, 0, 24000, 0], [19, 1, 20.0, 20.071, 20.672, 0, 24000, 0], [20, 1, 21.0, 21.071, 21.66, 0, 24000, 0], [21, 1, 22.0, 22.074, 22.702, 0, 24000, 0], [22, 1, 23.0, 23.075, 23.663, 0, 24000, 0], [23, 1, 24.0, 24.068, 24.571, 0, 24000, 0], [24, 1, 25.0, 25.068, 25.63, 0, 24000, 0], [25, 1, 26.0, 26.066, 26.556, 0, 24000, 0], [26, 1, 27.0, 27.066, 27.673, 0, 24000, 0], [27, 1, 28.0, 28.075, 28.698, 0, 24000, 0], [28, 1, 29.0, 29.076, 29.702, 0, 24000, 0], [29, 1, 30.0, 30.